"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[6231],{36231:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"schemaVersion":1,"locale":"en","slug":"06-strategiya","title":"Lesson 06. Strategy: Architecture of a Calm Portfolio","sourceFingerprint":"800a269023d03d468cf990a95a33955fc04ed298fe7ec35bfbf24fdf044f0c1d","blocks":[{"id":"b001","markdown":"*MakeCash Book \xb7 Learning Block 6*  \\n*Reading time: ~7 minutes*"},{"id":"b002","markdown":"# Architecture of a Calm Portfolio: Why Composure Beats Hustle"},{"id":"b003","markdown":"> \\"Wealth in the stock market is formed not by the speed of clicking in an app, but by the ability to not interfere with compound interest doing its work for years.\\""},{"id":"b004","markdown":"## 1. The Harsh Arithmetic of Retail Trading: Lessons from Statistics"},{"id":"b005","markdown":"In mid-2026, the regulator (Bank of Russia) summarized the results of a three-year period (2023–2025), analyzing the actual returns of retail brokerage accounts ranging from 10,000 to 10,000,000 ₽. The outcome was discouraging: **the average retail investor achieved a mere 2.9% per annum**."},{"id":"b006","markdown":"For comparison: the most conservative instrument—a standard bank deposit—yielded an average of **13.7% per annum** over the same period."},{"id":"b007","markdown":"A breakdown by trader category revealed the following distribution:\\n* **41% of participants** recorded a modest result ranging from **0.1% to 6.7% per year**;\\n* **13% of accounts** showed growth between **6.7% and 17.2%**;\\n* **Only 9% of investors** managed to cross the threshold of **17.2% per annum**;\\n* **About 37% of portfolios** hovered around the zero mark or plunged into deep drawdown."},{"id":"b008","markdown":"As expected, the primary destroyer of capital was speculative trading in derivatives (futures and options) and attempts to predict short-term swings."},{"id":"b009","markdown":"At the opposite extreme were holders of ETF and mutual fund units: their average growth rate was **17.9% per annum**. To be sure, the 2023–2025 period was marked by a tight monetary policy cycle, when money market funds (such as LQDT) delivered record yields amid a high key rate. Nevertheless, the gap between the **2.9%** earned by active traders and the **17.9%** earned by passive fund owners is fundamental: it cannot be written off as pure luck."},{"id":"b010","markdown":"## 2. \\"The Behavioral Gap\\": Why the Fund Grows While the Investor Loses"},{"id":"b011","markdown":"An exchange-traded fund operates on the principle of a collective trust. Capital from hundreds of thousands of investors flows into a single pool, with which a professional manager purchases a targeted basket of assets strictly according to regulations: government bonds, broad market index equities, or physical gold. Each participant owns a unit—a legally distinct fractional share of the shared pool. The fund\'s assets are insulated from the management company\'s balance sheet, and trades are executed in seconds via an exchange terminal."},{"id":"b012","markdown":"Yet a paradox emerges: the fund itself can deliver stellar performance over a decade, while the average person investing in it ends up with a loss."},{"id":"b013","markdown":"The analytical firm *Morningstar* regularly releases its *\\"Mind the Gap\\"* study, comparing the net returns of funds with the actual financial outcomes of their investors:"},{"id":"b014","markdown":"* Over the ten-year period ending in late 2024, American individual investors trailed the performance of the funds they held by an average of **1.2 percentage points annually**.\\n* Due to poor timing, investors voluntarily burned away roughly **15% of total returns**."},{"id":"b015","markdown":"### Anatomy of a Typical Emotional Mistake\\n1. **A fund surges by 30% in a year.** Glowing forecasts appear everywhere, and charts light up in green. The retail crowd gives in to herd mentality and pours savings in at the absolute market peak.\\n2. **A 20% market correction strikes.** Panic sets in. Unable to handle the stress of the drawdown, the investor dumps units at a loss.\\n3. **Cycle outcome:** the underlying instrument appreciated, but the emotional owner locked in a permanent loss."},{"id":"b016","markdown":"> \uD83D\uDCCC **Important Morningstar observation:** In balanced index funds, where the target mix of equities and bonds is maintained automatically, this gap shrinks to zero. When asset allocation rules are established in advance, investors are spared the temptation to react impulsively to news headlines."},{"id":"b017","markdown":"Attempting to overhaul asset allocation on a weekly basis is like a novice gardener pulling a sapling out of the dirt every morning to check if its roots have grown stronger. The plant will inevitably die from such \\"care.\\" Exactly the same thing happens to an investment portfolio."},{"id":"b018","markdown":"## 3. The Hidden Enemies of Compound Interest"},{"id":"b019","markdown":"### Marketing Traps of Brokerage Interfaces\\nA brokerage firm earns its core revenue from two sources: **turnover commissions** and **interest charged on margin leverage**. The more frequently a client trades, the wealthier the broker becomes."},{"id":"b020","markdown":"This is precisely why apps are engineered around the mechanics of a casino:\\n* intrusive push notifications (\\"Stock X jumped 7% in an hour!\\");\\n* flashy dashboards showcasing \\"Top Gainers of the Day\\";\\n* curated lists of speculative \\"investment ideas\\" compiled by in-house analysts."},{"id":"b021","markdown":"All of these are digital engagement tools prompting excessive actions."},{"id":"b022","markdown":"**Digital hygiene rule:** remove the trading app from your smartphone\'s home screen, mute all price alerts, and set a strict schedule for logging into the terminal — **once a month** to deposit funds and **once a year** to check the balance."},{"id":"b023","markdown":"### Tax Erosion of Capital\\nLocking in profits on a trade instantly turns 13% of earnings into an obligation to the state budget (personal income tax). This money is irrevocably removed from the compounding base."},{"id":"b024","markdown":"An investor who holds assets for years without selling preserves the principal in full, and when holding securities for more than three years gains the right to a legal tax exemption (long-term holding relief — LDV)."},{"id":"b025","markdown":"### The Destructive Power of Management Fees (TER)\\nAny fund issuer deducts a percentage for administration. A difference of tenths of a percent over a quarter-century horizon turns into a financial abyss."},{"id":"b026","markdown":"## \uD83E\uDDEE Back-of-the-envelope calculation: the price of a tenth of a percent"},{"id":"b027","markdown":"Imagine a disciplined investor who allocates **10 000 ₽ every month** to the stock market for **25 years** (total personal contributions will amount to **3 000 000 ₽**)."},{"id":"b028","markdown":"Suppose the underlying index yields **10% per annum** before deducting fee expenses."},{"id":"b029","markdown":"Let\'s see what capital accumulates in the account depending on the fund\'s total expense ratio (management company fee, specialized depository, exchange fees):"},{"id":"b030","markdown":"| Annual total fee | Final capital after 25 years | Real loss to fees |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **0.5%** | **~12.3 million ₽** | Baseline benchmark of an efficient fund |\\n| **1.0%** | **~11.3 million ₽** | Loss of **1.0 million ₽** relative to the cheap fund |\\n| **2.0%** | **~9.6 million ₽** | Loss of **2.7 million ₽** of capital |\\n| **3.0%** | **~8.2 million ₽** | Loss of **4.1 million ₽** (more than all personal contributions!) |"},{"id":"b031","markdown":"```\\nComparison of financial results after a quarter-century:\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\nPersonal contributions:  3.0 mln ₽\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\nFund with 0.5% fee:     12.3 mln ₽  ████████████████████████████\\nFund with 3.0% fee:      8.2 mln ₽  ██████████████████\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\nDifference (lost profit): 4.1 mln ₽ given away to intermediaries!\\n```"},{"id":"b032","markdown":"> ⚠️ The gap between a high-quality exchange-traded fund (0.5%) and an inefficient retail mutual fund (3%) exceeds **4 000 000 ₽**. This is an amount exceeding all your savings over 25 years of hard work."},{"id":"b033","markdown":"It is precisely costs that explain the failure of institutional managers: according to S&P reports (SPIVA research), by the end of 2024, **89.5% of actively managed US equity funds underperformed the benchmark S&P 500 over a 15-year period**. Managers may pick assets professionally, but the burden of internal fees inevitably drags them to the bottom."},{"id":"b034","markdown":"## 4. Passive Investor Algorithm"},{"id":"b035","markdown":"Five steps are enough to build an antifragile portfolio:"},{"id":"b036","markdown":"1. **Fix target asset class allocations.** Based on your horizon and risk profile, allocate the portfolio (for example: 40% in protective liquidity and bonds, 60% in business equity assets).\\n2. **Use exchange-traded funds instead of single issuers.** Form a core of 3–4 low-cost instruments: the MOEX equity index, a basket of reliable OFZ, a money market fund (LQDT), and an instrument pegged to physical gold.\\n3. **Set up regular automatic payments.** Transfer a fixed amount right on payday. When prices drop, you automatically buy more units at a bargain price; when they rise, you buy fewer. The need to time the market disappears.\\n4. **Rebalance once every 12 months.** Buying laggard assets once a year is enough to restore allocations to target weights.\\n5. **Do not touch the portfolio in between.** Ignore economic headlines and market fluctuations."},{"id":"b037","markdown":"### What to do if there is an irresistible urge to trade?\\nIf you are genuinely interested in picking individual stocks, set aside a **sandbox (hobby account)** for this:\\n* Cap its size at an amount you wouldn\'t mind spending on extreme entertainment like skydiving (no more than 5–10% of your savings).\\n* Forbid yourself from depositing additional funds into this account during the year if the balance is wiped out.\\n* Compare the results of your speculations annually against the performance of a classic index fund. The actual numbers will quickly put everything into perspective."},{"id":"b038","markdown":"## \uD83D\uDCCB Practical Assignment"},{"id":"b039","markdown":"1. Go to your smartphone\'s system settings and completely **turn off banners and notifications** from brokerage apps.\\n2. Download the broker statement for the past calendar year and write down on a sheet of paper the **exact amount of trading commissions paid**.\\n3. Check the fact sheet of each fund in your portfolio. If the total expense ratio (TER) exceeds **1.2–1.5% per year**, find a low-cost exchange-traded alternative on MOEX."}],"terms":[{"id":"investorskiy-razryv","term":"Investor Gap","definition":"the loss of return incurred by an individual investor due to attempts to time market peaks."},{"id":"komissiya-ter","term":"Fund Expense Ratio (TER)","definition":"the total annual expenses of a management company deducted from the net asset value of the fund\'s units."},{"id":"passivnyj-investor","term":"Passive investor","definition":"an investor who purchases the broad market and holds it without speculating."}],"quizzes":[{"id":"q1","q":"According to regulator data for the three-year cycle of 2023–2025, the average return of retail brokerage accounts was 2.9% annualized, whereas fund units grew by 17.9%. What was the primary reason active individual investors lagged behind?","options":["Moscow Exchange charged increased fees exclusively from retail accounts.","Retail investors extensively overused derivatives and poorly timed speculative trades, while passive funds held assets and earned money market rates.","The Bank of Russia froze dividend payments on citizens\' brokerage accounts."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Active speculative trading in derivatives and emotional market entry and exit slashed the returns of retail accounts, while funds collected coupon income and the high risk-free money market premium."},{"id":"q2","q":"Morningstar research identifies an \'Investor Gap,\' which causes clients to earn on average 1.2 percentage points per year less than the instrument they invested in. What causes this loss?","options":["Unscrupulous fund managers deliberately underreport actual performance figures to investors.","Technical rounding of quotes occurs during unit splits on the exchange.","Investors succumb to greed and fear: buying additional assets after a massive rally and panic selling them after a drawdown."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Irrational behavioral timing forces people to enter the market with maximum capital at an overheated peak and exit to cash at the very bottom of a decline."},{"id":"q3","q":"Two investors contribute 10,000 ₽ monthly for 25 years with market growth of 10% per year. The first chose an exchange-traded fund with a fee of 0.5% per year, while the second chose a retail mutual fund with costs of 3% per year. What is the difference in final capital between them?","options":["The difference will not exceed 200,000 ₽, as 2.5% is a negligible discrepancy.","The final capital will be identical, since fees are deducted only when closing the account.","The difference will exceed 4 million ₽ in favor of the low-cost fund, which is more than the total sum of their personal contributions over 25 years."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Due to the effect of compound interest, a high management fee eats up more than 4.1 million ₽ of future capital value, reducing accumulated savings from 12.3 million ₽ to just 8.2 million ₽."}],"officialSources":[]}')}}]);